<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one, and suppose f times f star equals u squared. Let I contain the distinct primes dividing u. Our evaluated values belong to B, the integers with i and the chosen prime roots of unity adjoined. Fix one prime p in I. Pair each subset S not containing p with the subset obtained by adding p. The same alternating product becomes a product of paired ratios, raised to alternating positive and negative first powers.<break time="700ms"/>For two distinct primes, p and q, move the four evaluations into two ratios in the p direction. The second ratio is inverted. Every factor keeps its original numerator or denominator role. We are regrouping one fixed number, Delta of f.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>At every maximal ideal above p, the preceding local comparison makes each paired ratio a unit with residue one. A unit has an inverse in the same local ring. Products and inverses preserve both being a unit and having residue one. Thus Delta has both properties there.<break time="700ms"/>Change the pairing direction to q. The grouping changes, but Delta does not. Repeat for every prime dividing u, and at every maximal ideal above that prime. Keep this residue-one information for the later restriction on the order.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Now consider a maximal ideal containing none of the primes dividing u. In this local ring, u squared is invertible. The evaluated norm identity gives an explicit inverse for each value: its complex conjugate divided by u squared. Multiplying the value by this inverse gives one. Every evaluated factor is therefore a unit here as well, so the alternating product has no denominator obstruction. Together, the two cases put Delta in B localized at every maximal ideal.<break time="1100ms"/></p>
<p>Why do the local checks give a global element of B? Write z for Delta, and suppose z is outside B. Form the ideal J of all elements b in B for which b times z belongs to B. This ideal is proper: if it contained one, z would already be in B. Choose a maximal ideal containing J.<break time="700ms"/>Local membership writes z as a divided by s, with a and s in B and s outside that maximal ideal. But s times z equals a. Therefore s clears z, so s belongs to J, inside the very ideal it was required to avoid. This contradiction proves Delta belongs to B.<break time="700ms"/>The generators of B are roots of unity, hence algebraic integers. Sums and products of algebraic integers remain algebraic integers, so Delta is an algebraic integer. Separately, we already proved that all its conjugates have magnitude one. These are the two inputs for Kronecker's theorem. Keep the local residue-one result too; the order restriction still comes later.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
